Network Simulation Environment and Configuration

1. Introduction

This research project investigates the performance of First-In-First-Out (FIFO), Priority Queuing (PQ), and Weighted Fair Queuing (WFQ) scheduling algorithms under simulated bandwidth-constrained network conditions.

A controlled network simulation environment will be developed to evaluate how the three algorithms manage competing traffic flows when available bandwidth is limited.

The simulation will use traffic characteristics extracted from the MAWI Working Group Traffic Archive.

2. Selection of Simulation Environment

The group will evaluate suitable network simulation tools before selecting the environment for implementation.

Potential tools include:

NS-3: A discrete-event network simulator that supports detailed modelling of network traffic, communication links and queueing behaviour.

Mininet: A network emulation platform that creates virtual hosts, switches and links for controlled networking experiments.

Python-based simulation: A custom discrete-event simulator that can model packet arrivals, queues, scheduling decisions and packet departures.

The selected environment must support:

- Packet arrival and transmission modelling.
- Configurable link bandwidth.
- Queue capacity limits.
- FIFO, PQ and WFQ scheduling behaviour.
- Packet-level performance measurements.
- Reproducible experimental configurations.

Selected tool: To be confirmed after technical evaluation.

3. Proposed Network Topology

The experiment will use a simplified network topology consisting of traffic sources, a scheduling router, a constrained communication link and a receiving endpoint.

Traffic Sources → Scheduling Router → Bandwidth-Constrained Link → Destination

Multiple competing traffic flows will enter the scheduling router.

The scheduling algorithm will determine the order in which packets are transmitted across the constrained link.

4. Proposed Simulation Parameters

The following parameters are proposed as initial experimental settings and must be validated before implementation.

Parameter| Proposed Configuration
Network topology| Multiple sources, one bottleneck link, one destination
Bandwidth| 2 Mbps, 5 Mbps and 10 Mbps
Scheduling algorithms| FIFO, PQ and WFQ
Traffic input| Prepared MAWI packet traffic traces
Queue capacity| 100 packets initially
Queue overflow policy| Tail drop
Simulation duration| Based on selected trace and queue-drain policy
Traffic classes| To be defined
WFQ weights| To be defined
Performance metrics| Throughput, delay, packet loss and fairness

The bandwidth values are experimental assumptions, not measured characteristics of rural Zimbabwean networks.

5. Bandwidth-Constrained Network Configuration

The simulation will introduce a bottleneck link with limited transmission capacity.

When the traffic arrival rate exceeds the available link capacity, packets may accumulate in the scheduler's queue.

If the queue reaches its configured capacity, additional packets will be dropped according to the selected overflow policy.

The group will evaluate how the scheduling algorithms manage these conditions.

6. Scheduling Algorithm Configuration

6.1 FIFO

FIFO will use a single queue.

Packets will be transmitted in arrival order, subject to the configured queue capacity and transmission rate.

6.2 Priority Queuing

PQ will use multiple queues assigned different priority levels.

Packets from the highest-priority non-empty queue will be served first.

Traffic classification rules and queue capacity allocation will be documented.

6.3 Weighted Fair Queuing

WFQ will use flow-based scheduling with assigned weights.

The weights will influence the relative service allocation among competing traffic flows.

The implementation must be verified to ensure that it follows weighted fair queueing principles rather than simple round-robin scheduling.

7. Experimental Consistency

To ensure a valid comparison, the following parameters will be controlled:

- Traffic input and packet arrival timing.
- Bottleneck bandwidth.
- Total queue capacity or buffering budget.
- Simulation duration and measurement interval.
- Packet drop policy.
- Traffic classification assumptions.
- Random seeds, where applicable.

Only the scheduling algorithm and its necessary algorithm-specific configuration will vary between corresponding experiments.

8. Simulation Verification

Before collecting performance results, the group will verify that:

1. Packets are transmitted according to the selected scheduling algorithm.
2. The configured bandwidth limit is enforced.
3. Queue capacity restrictions are applied.
4. Packet drops are recorded correctly.
5. Packet transmission and reception timestamps are available.
6. The experiment can be repeated using documented settings.

9. Expected Outcome

The expected outcome is a documented, reproducible network simulation configuration suitable for comparing FIFO, PQ and WFQ under bandwidth-constrained conditions.

The environment will provide a controlled basis for evaluating throughput, packet delay, packet loss and flow fairness.
